// A local declared in one block is gone when the block closes — a later SIBLING block may bind the
// same name and gets ITS type (consumer KB-20).
//
// The emitter's per-name local tables were flat maps that `popScope` never touched, so `string m` here
// stayed typed to the end of `main`. A `match` payload binding does not write `_localCTypes` (by design:
// that map drives ownership detection), so `lvalueCType` — which `typeOfExpr` consults FIRST — answered
// the stale `kama_string` for the binding, and the call below was rejected with "argument `v` expects a
// type value, so it cannot be given a `string`": valid code refused, diagnosed at the wrong line, naming
// neither the binding nor the block the stale type came from. Neither block encloses the other, so there
// is nothing here to shadow; the SPEC says sibling scopes may reuse a name freely.
//
// Fixed at the root: a `Scope` owns the table entries its binders displace and restores them on pop.
type value E { public int32 n; public ctor of(int32 n) { this.n = n; } }
fn int32 takeE(E v) { return v.n; }
fn int32 main() {
    Optional<E> inner = Optional::Some(value: E.of(n: 5));
    int32 r = 0;
    if (r == 1) {
        string m = "boom";                    // sibling block — does NOT enclose the one below
        r = cast<int32>(m.length());
    } else {
        match (inner) { case Some(value: m): { r = takeE(v: m); } case None: { } };
    }
    return r;   // 5
}
